                    "value": "Test Planning & Test Case Design"
                },
                {
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "value": "A game without a test plan is like a dungeon without a map — you will wander aimlessly, miss critical paths, and probably fall into a pit. Test planning is the discipline of systematically deciding what to test, how to test it, and when testing is 'done.' Both testers and developers benefit from understanding test planning: testers execute the plan, and developers use it to understand what behavior is expected vs. what is broken.\n\n**The Test Plan** is a living document that answers:\n• What features/systems need testing?\n• What types of testing will be performed (functional, performance, compatibility, etc.)?\n• What are the entry and exit criteria for each testing phase?\n• What resources (hardware, time, people) are needed?\n• What risks could derail testing, and what is the mitigation plan?"
                },
                {
                    "type": "heading",
                    "level": "h3",
                    "value": "The Anatomy of a Good Test Case"
                },
                {
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "value": "A test case is a single, atomic instruction that verifies one specific behavior. Good test cases are unambiguous, independent, and verifiable. They follow a standard structure:\n\n**Test Case ID**: Unique identifier (e.g., TC_COMBAT_001)\n**Title**: Brief description of what is being tested\n**Preconditions**: What must be true before the test starts\n**Steps**: Numbered, exact actions to perform\n**Expected Result**: What should happen if the feature works correctly\n**Actual Result**: What actually happened (filled during execution)\n**Status**: Pass / Fail / Blocked / Skipped\n**Environment**: Build version, platform, hardware specs"
                },
                {
                    "type": "code",
                    "language": "csharp",
                    "value": "// Example: A C# test case validator that checks if a test case object is well-formed\n// This is useful for test management tools built in Unity\n\nusing System;\nusing System.Collections.Generic;\nusing UnityEngine;\n\n[Serializable]\npublic class TestCase\n{\n    public string testCaseId;\n    public string title;\n    public string feature;\n    public List<string> preconditions;\n    public List<string> steps;\n    public string expectedResult;\n    public string actualResult;\n    public TestStatus status;\n    public string environment;\n    public string assignedTester;\n    public DateTime createdDate;\n    public DateTime? executedDate;\n\n    public enum TestStatus\n    {\n        NotRun,\n        InProgress,\n        Passed,\n        Failed,\n        Blocked,\n        Skipped\n    }\n}\n\npublic static class TestCaseValidator\n{\n    public static List<string> Validate(TestCase testCase)\n    {\n        var errors = new List<string>();\n\n        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(testCase.testCaseId))\n            errors.Add(\"Test Case ID is required\");\n\n        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(testCase.title))\n            errors.Add(\"Title is required\");\n\n        if (testCase.preconditions == null || testCase.preconditions.Count == 0)\n            errors.Add(\"At least one precondition is required\");\n\n        if (testCase.steps == null || testCase.steps.Count == 0)\n            errors.Add(\"At least one step is required\");\n\n        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(testCase.expectedResult))\n            errors.Add(\"Expected Result is required\");\n\n        // Check for vague expected results\n        var vagueTerms = new[] { \"works\", \"functions\", \"ok\", \"fine\" };\n        foreach (var term in vagueTerms)\n        {\n            if (testCase.expectedResult?.ToLower().Contains(term) == true)\n                errors.Add($\"Expected Result is too vague. Avoid using '{term}'. Be specific.\");\n        }\n\n        return errors;\n    }\n}\n\n// Usage example\npublic class TestCaseExample : MonoBehaviour\n{\n    void Start()\n    {\n        var testCase = new TestCase\n        {\n            testCaseId = \"TC_COMBAT_001\",\n            title = \"Player takes damage from enemy melee attack\",\n            feature = \"Combat System\",\n            preconditions = new List<string>\n            {\n                \"Player has 100 HP\",\n                \"Enemy is within melee range (2 units)\",\n                \"Game is in Play Mode\"\n            },\n            steps = new List<string>\n            {\n                \"Spawn player at origin (0, 0, 0)\",\n                \"Spawn enemy at (2, 0, 0) facing player\",\n                \"Trigger enemy attack animation\",\n                \"Wait for attack hit frame (frame 12)\",\n                \"Read player current HP\"\n            },\n            expectedResult = \"Player HP decreases by exactly 25. HP bar UI updates to show 75/100. Damage number '25' appears above player. Screen flashes red briefly.\",\n            environment = \"Unity 2022.3.20f1, Windows 11, DX12, RTX 3060\"\n        };\n\n        var errors = TestCaseValidator.Validate(testCase);\n        if (errors.Count > 0)\n        {\n            Debug.LogError(\"Test Case Validation Failed:\");\n            foreach (var error in errors)\n                Debug.LogError($\"  - {error}\");\n        }\n        else\n        {\n            Debug.Log(\"✅ Test Case TC_COMBAT_001 is valid and ready for execution\");\n        }\n    }\n}",
                    "output": "✅ Test Case TC_COMBAT_001 is valid and ready for execution\n\nIf the expected result was 'Player takes damage ok' instead, the validator would output:\nTest Case Validation Failed:\n  - Expected Result is too vague. Avoid using 'ok'. Be specific."
