                    "type": "heading",
                    "level": "h3",
                    "value": "Test Case Design Techniques"
                },
                {
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "value": "Writing effective test cases requires technique, not just intuition. Here are proven methods used in professional game QA:\n\n**Equivalence Partitioning**: Divide input ranges into groups that should behave the same way. Test one value from each partition.\n• Example: Player level 1-10 (novice), 11-30 (intermediate), 31-50 (advanced), 51+ (master). Test level 5, 20, 40, and 60.\n\n**Boundary Value Analysis**: Test at the edges of partitions — this is where bugs lurk.\n• Example: Test level exactly 10, exactly 11, exactly 30, exactly 31, exactly 50, exactly 51.\n\n**State Transition Testing**: Games are state machines. Test every valid and invalid transition.\n• Example: Player states: Idle → Walking → Running → Jumping → Falling → Landing → Idle. Test jumping from idle (valid), jumping while falling (invalid), landing while in air (valid).\n\n**Decision Table Testing**: For complex rule systems, create a table of all condition combinations.\n• Example: Critical hit depends on (attacker luck > threshold) AND (defender is flanked) AND (weapon has crit bonus). Test all 8 combinations of these booleans.\n\n**Pairwise Testing**: When there are too many combinations, test every pair of values at least once. Tools like PICT (Microsoft) can generate these efficiently."
                },
