                    "type": "heading",
                    "level": "h3",
                    "value": "Test Coverage: How Much is Enough?"
                },
                {
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "value": "Test coverage measures how much of the game has been tested. There are several types:\n\n**Code Coverage**: What percentage of lines/branches in the codebase were executed by tests? Tools like OpenCover or Unity's built-in coverage package can measure this. Aim for 70-80% for game logic, but do not chase 100% — some code (rendering, animation) is better tested visually.\n\n**Feature Coverage**: What percentage of designed features have test cases? A feature matrix maps every feature to its test cases.\n\n**Risk-Based Coverage**: Test more where risk is highest. A save system gets more coverage than a credits screen. Multiplayer netcode gets more than single-player UI.\n\n**For Developers**: Write code with coverage in mind. If a function has 12 branches, that is 12 test cases minimum. Consider simplifying complex logic to reduce the testing burden.\n\n**For Testers**: Track coverage metrics and report gaps. 'We have 200 test cases for combat but only 5 for the save system' is a valuable insight that prevents ship-blocking bugs."
                },
                {
                    "type": "heading",
                    "level": "h3",
                    "value": "The Test Plan Template for Unity Games"
                },
                {
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "value": "Here is a practical test plan template structure for a Unity game project:\n\n**1. Introduction**\n• Project name, version, target platforms\n• Testing scope (what is in scope, what is out of scope)\n• References (design docs, GDD, technical specs)\n\n**2. Test Strategy**\n• Testing types: Unit, Integration, Functional, Performance, Compatibility, Compliance\n• Testing phases: Alpha (feature-complete, buggy), Beta (content-complete, polish), RC (release candidate, final validation)\n• Entry criteria: When can testing begin? (build is stable, features are implemented, test cases are written)\n• Exit criteria: When is testing done? (all P0/P1 bugs fixed, performance targets met, compliance passed)\n\n**3. Test Environment**\n• Hardware configurations (minimum, recommended, high-end)\n• Software versions (Unity version, OS versions, graphics drivers)\n• Network conditions (for multiplayer: LAN, WiFi, 4G, packet loss simulation)\n\n**4. Schedule & Resources**\n• Testing timeline aligned with development milestones\n• Tester assignments and responsibilities\n• Risk assessment and contingency plans\n\n**5. Deliverables**\n• Test cases (spreadsheet or test management tool)\n• Bug reports (Jira, Azure DevOps, etc.)\n• Test summary report (pass/fail rates, coverage metrics, open bug counts)\n• Performance benchmark reports"
