                    "type": "heading",
                    "level": "h2",
                    "value": "Unity Play Mode vs Edit Mode Testing"
                },
                {
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "value": "Unity's Test Framework provides two distinct testing environments: **Edit Mode** and **Play Mode**. Understanding when to use each is fundamental to effective Unity testing. Both testers and developers must grasp this distinction — testers need to know which mode to run tests in, and developers need to write code that is testable in the appropriate mode.\n\n**Edit Mode Tests** run inside the Unity Editor without entering Play Mode. They execute in the Editor's domain, have access to Editor APIs, and are extremely fast. They are ideal for testing pure C# logic, data structures, algorithms, and Editor tools.\n\n**Play Mode Tests** run inside a temporary Play Mode session. They execute in the Player loop, have access to runtime APIs (MonoBehaviour lifecycle, physics, rendering), and are slower because they must start/stop Play Mode. They are essential for testing gameplay behavior, physics interactions, and scene-based logic."
                },
                {
                    "type": "heading",
                    "level": "h3",
                    "value": "Edit Mode Tests: Fast, Isolated, Logic-Focused"
                },
                {
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "value": "Edit Mode tests are your first line of defense. They run in milliseconds, require no scene setup, and can be executed continuously during development.\n\n**When to use Edit Mode**:\n• Testing pure C# classes (no MonoBehaviour dependency)\n• Testing algorithms (pathfinding, sorting, damage calculation)\n• Testing data validation (JSON parsing, save file formats)\n• Testing Editor tools and custom inspectors\n• Testing ScriptableObject data\n\n**Limitations**:\n• Cannot test MonoBehaviour lifecycle methods (Start, Update, OnCollisionEnter)\n• Cannot test physics (Rigidbody, Collider interactions)\n• Cannot test rendering or visual output\n• Cannot test coroutines or time-dependent behavior\n\n**Best Practice**: Extract as much logic as possible into pure C# classes. A `DamageCalculator` class can be fully tested in Edit Mode, while the `CombatController` MonoBehaviour that uses it is tested in Play Mode."
