                },
                {
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "value": "Play Mode tests are necessary when you need to test behavior that depends on Unity's runtime systems. They are slower but essential for gameplay validation.\n\n**When to use Play Mode**:\n• Testing MonoBehaviour lifecycle (Start, Update, OnEnable, OnDisable)\n• Testing physics interactions (collisions, triggers, raycasts)\n• Testing coroutines and time-dependent behavior (yield return new WaitForSeconds)\n• Testing scene loading and object instantiation\n• Testing UnityEvents, animations, and particle systems\n• Testing integration between multiple systems\n\n**Performance Considerations**:\n• Play Mode tests start/stop Play Mode for each test fixture, which takes 1-3 seconds.\n• Use [UnitySetUp] and [UnityTearDown] to share scene setup across tests in a fixture.\n• Use [PrebuildSetup] to prepare assets before tests run.\n• Avoid testing visual output — use programmatic assertions instead.\n\n**For Developers**: Design your MonoBehaviours to be testable. Expose key state through public properties or events so tests can verify behavior without relying on visual inspection."
