                {
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "value": "Proper folder organization is critical for Unity tests to compile and run correctly. Unity uses **Assembly Definition Files** (.asmdef) to control which code can reference which other code.\n\n**Recommended Folder Structure**:\n```\nAssets/\n├── Scripts/                    # Production code\n│   ├── Gameplay/\n│   ├── UI/\n│   └── Utils/\n│   └── Scripts.asmdef          # Production assembly\n├── Tests/\n│   ├── EditMode/               # Edit Mode tests\n│   │   ├── Gameplay/\n│   │   ├── Utils/\n│   │   └── Tests.EditMode.asmdef\n│   └── PlayMode/               # Play Mode tests\n│       ├── Gameplay/\n│       ├── UI/\n│       └── Tests.PlayMode.asmdef\n```\n\n**Assembly Definition Setup**:\n• `Scripts.asmdef`: References UnityEngine, UnityEditor (if needed), and any packages.\n• `Tests.EditMode.asmdef`: References `Scripts.asmdef`, `UnityEngine.TestRunner`, `UnityEditor.TestRunner`. Set **Platform Settings** → **Editor only**.\n• `Tests.PlayMode.asmdef`: References `Scripts.asmdef`, `UnityEngine.TestRunner`. Set **Platform Settings** → **Any Platform**.\n\n**Critical**: Tests cannot reference production code unless the test assembly references the production assembly. If you see 'type or namespace name could not be found' errors, check your assembly references.\n\n**For Developers**: Keep production code in its own assembly, separate from tests. This enforces clean architecture — production code should never reference test code, but test code can reference production code."
                },
                {
                    "type": "image",
                    "src": "https://example.com/unity-test-folder-structure.png",
                    "alt": "Unity Test Folder Structure with Assembly Definitions",
                    "caption": "Recommended folder structure and assembly definition setup for Unity testing projects"
                },
                {
                    "type": "comment",
                    "value": "IMAGE TYPE: File tree diagram — A clean, hierarchical file tree showing the recommended Unity project structure for testing. Use a VS Code-style explorer view with folder icons. Show: Assets folder expanded with Scripts (blue folder icon) and Tests (green folder icon). Scripts contains Gameplay, UI, Utils subfolders and Scripts.asmdef. Tests contains EditMode and PlayMode subfolders, each with their own .asmdef files. Use different colors for production (blue) and test (green) assemblies. Show arrows indicating assembly references: Tests.EditMode.asmdef → Scripts.asmdef, Tests.PlayMode.asmdef → Scripts.asmdef. Include a small Inspector panel mockup showing the .asmdef reference settings. Dark theme, professional Unity development aesthetic."
                },
                {
                    "type": "heading",
                    "level": "h3",
                    "value": "Common Pitfalls & Best Practices"
                },
                {
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "value": "**Pitfall 1: Testing private fields via reflection**\nWhile the Play Mode example above uses reflection to read private fields, this is fragile. Better approaches:\n• Make fields `internal` and use `[assembly: InternalsVisibleTo(\"Tests.PlayMode\")]`\n• Expose read-only properties: `public int CurrentHealth { get; private set; }`\n• Create test-specific subclasses or interfaces\n\n**Pitfall 2: Scene dependencies in tests**\nTests that require specific scene objects are brittle. Use programmatic object creation (as shown above) or prefab instantiation.\n\n**Pitfall 3: Time-dependent tests**\nTests using `WaitForSeconds` or frame counts are flaky. Use `Time.timeScale` manipulation or mock time providers.\n\n**Pitfall 4: Not cleaning up after tests**\nAlways use `[UnityTearDown]` to destroy test objects. Leftover objects pollute subsequent tests and the Editor.\n\n**Best Practice: The Arrange-Act-Assert Pattern**\nEvery test should have three clear sections:\n• **Arrange**: Set up the test scenario (create objects, set state)\n• **Act**: Execute the behavior being tested\n• **Assert**: Verify the outcome matches expectations\n\nThis pattern makes tests readable, maintainable, and self-documenting."
                },
